<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Spec section 20: no GPL dependency in the default build, ever (LIC-1). Every
    entry added here carries a licence note. Where GPL functionality is genuinely
    wanted it is invoked out of process as a tool the user supplies (RND-9,
    RND-15), never referenced from this file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The official MCP C# SDK, which section 20's dependency table names and
      marks "verify current version and licence". Verified: 2.2.0 declares
      Apache-2.0 as an SPDX expression in its own nuspec, and its whole
      transitive closure is ten Microsoft.Extensions.* packages, all MIT.

      The .Core package rather than the full one: it carries the server, the
      tool primitives and the stdio transport, and leaves behind the
      Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting integration this does not use.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol.Core" Version="2.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The 3-D viewport. Section 16 names Helix Toolkit on its DirectX 11 path and says
      why: plain WPF Media3D cannot render 10^4 trajectories interactively.

      Verified rather than assumed, per section 20's "verify current version and
      licence" and r06's own caveat that third-party status must be re-checked before
      being committed to. 3.1.2 is MIT, read from the LICENSE file embedded in the
      package rather than from a URL, and its transitive closure is MIT throughout -
      CommunityToolkit, Cyotek, Microsoft.Extensions, and SharpDX. LIC-1 clear.

      TWO THINGS THE RE-CHECK FOUND, both recorded as an amendment in SPEC.md.

      The version contemporaneous with r06 no longer works: 2.27.3 is .NET Framework
      4.x only and restores on net10 solely through the NU1701 compatibility shim.
      3.1.2 restores cleanly, and is why this is pinned to the 3.x line.

      And every Helix DirectX package depends on SharpDX, ARCHIVED SINCE DECEMBER 2020.
      Taken knowingly. The blast radius is small by design: section 17 makes the
      interactive viewport "screen tuning, not an artifact", so publication figures come
      out of Einzel.Render - vector, headless, no third-party dependency. If SharpDX
      breaks on some future Windows we lose interactive tumbling and lose no figure, no
      number and no test. Vortice.Windows is the maintained successor and the exit if it
      ever bites; Helix has no Vortice backend today.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="HelixToolkit.Wpf.SharpDX" Version="3.1.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Test stack. MIT / Apache-2.0. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
